  "idPrefix": "doodad.urban",
  "note": "This sheet is a 1254x1254 plate drawn to a stated 10x10 grid. It slices to one fused piece, so it was cut on that grid instead, and the entries below are its cells in reading order - left to right, then top to bottom. Cut once, then annotate.\n\nTHE SHEET CARRIES ITS OWN ALPHA. It arrived RGB and fully opaque, and the alpha that makes a crate a crate instead of a black square lived only in the shipped atlas, which normalize_tilesets.py treated as this sheet's hard contract. It was keyed into the raw art once, as a reviewed edit: RGB is untouched and only the alpha channel was added. The key is per cell of the cut, because what counts as background is a fact about a cell rather than about the sheet - the same dark gutter is background beside a crate and is the edge of a floor tile.\n\nA cell's background is near-black, holds a pocket wider than the gutter, and is either reached from the cell's border, or large enough to be the cell's own emptiness, or within a few pixels of background that is. Each clause is there because dropping it broke a real piece on this sheet. Without the pocket width a floor tile keys to a grid of holes. Without 'the cell's own emptiness' the hollow centre of urban.wall exports solid. Without the last clause the chairs export with a brick between their legs: that gap is pure black but it is sealed at the bottom by the chair's own contact shadow, three pixels of 12-23, so a flood that only travels through near-black can never get into it. The flood travels through near-black and is grown a few pixels into merely-dark afterwards, which is what keeps a dark seam drawn across a prop - a crate's lid line - from being a corridor into its middle.\n\nThen dust is removed. The background carries specks above the key, and the cut leaves a sliver of the neighbouring cell along its edge; at 4:1 each survives the downscale as detached dirt beside the prop. Anything not joined to a piece's main body and either smaller or thinner than drawn art is dropped. The main body is never dropped however thin it is, and the rubble decals keep their second and third stones.\n\nMeasured against the hand-maintained atlas piece by piece: opacity mean 1.5 points, worst 8.5 (doodad.chest-2, whose generated cut is the tighter of the two); every void it leaves open is still open; detached area matches it everywhere except the rubble decals, which are within a few pixels. UrbanTilesetAlphaTest pins all three. Note that opacity ALONE does not catch a filled pocket - plugging the chairs' leg gap and trimming their outline cancelled to -0.027, well inside tolerance, and the defect was found by looking at the picture.\n\nDO NOT RE-SLICE. Slicing now finds the pieces by alpha and would replace the cut cells with whatever the threshold sees, taking every block assignment and id with them. The re-slice guard refuses it; that refusal is correct and is not to be forced. Correct a cut with tileset_fit_grid, which moves the existing cells.\n\nCUT. The column axis is fitted to the sheet's own gutters (origin 2.31, pitch 123.25). The row axis is the plain canvas division (origin 0, pitch 125.40) because GridFit disowns its own row measurement and that refusal stands: the plate's bottom two rows are empty art, so only 7 of the 9 stated boundaries can land on a real feature and the fit needs 8. Keying the sheet improved the fit without changing that - the row residual fell from 6.0px to 4.2px, inside the 5.0px tolerance, while the boundary count is structural. The stated line is not far wrong in any case: measured against the sheet's own row gutters it sits within 5.4px, worst case, of where they are, against 6.2px for a line fitted to the art's outer bounds. What the row fit reports as 9.4px off is the distance to the CHANGE seams, which are the far edge of each gutter rather than its middle.\n\nIts 3x3 wall and floor patches are what block grouping was built for. urban.wall was checked against the art before it was kept: corner studs sit on the outer corners, each edge cell carries its face slit on the side the slot names as exterior, and the enclosed centre is blank because the 0x060A10 fill is what that case is for. urban.floor, urban.rubble and the single-cell urban.door-open were taken from the shipped tileset's own origins and layouts, and the wall assignment was independently re-derived from the layout and found to agree cell for cell.\n\nExporting repacks the atlas, so ids survive and coordinates do not. Anything addressing this sheet by a hardcoded (col,row) instead of an id breaks silently on every export - the map still draws, and simply draws the wrong thing. Three such references have been retired; the row-3 paving cells carry ids (doodad.fl-grate-1, doodad.fl-striped-yellow, doodad.fl-grate-2) because VehicleBayFitting paints bay decks with them.\n\nExcluded: the damaged wall 3x3 at cols 3-5 rows 4-6, the two computer-console wall cells, and rows 8-9. The shipped tileset never addressed any of them, so they are art the game has no name for. Group the damaged wall as a block if it is ever wanted - the art is there.",
  "sheet": "art-source/tilesets/urban-tileset.raw.png",
  "sheetName": "urban-tileset"
